  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Friday)\nIssued at 949 PM CST FRI NOV 6 2026\n\nCold air advection will continue overnight and tomorrow behind a\nsecondary cold front. A post-frontal stratus deck has slowed its\nsouthward advance as it reaches our far northern counties so far.\nWinds and the stratus deck should prevent frost formation\ntomorrow morning. Highs will be below normal on Saturday. High\npressure begins to move in Saturday night. Winds may stay up a\nlittle at unsheltered locations, but sheltered locations will see\nfavorable radiational cooling conditions. Winds will be even\nlighter/calm Sunday night. Chances for frost are medium to high\nacross the far northern counties and Black Belt Sunday and Monday\nmornings, with a low chance for a light freeze in the typically\ncolder locations. Frost advisories will likely be needed when\nthis gets closer.\n\nZonal flow will result in moderating temperatures early next\nweek. A moisture-starved shortwave trough moves eastward across\nthe area Tuesday/Tuesday night, which may result in a few\nsprinkles. Then a clipper system or two may squeeze out a few\nshowers Wednesday through Friday as a deep longwave trough\namplifies over the eastern CONUS. A strong cold front will result\nin low to medium chances for freezing temperatures next weekend.\n\n32/JDavis",
